-- Q3501: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A file system storing data in flat text files with no defined relationships","A spreadsheet application used primarily for financial analysis and modeling","A system organizing data into related tables using SQL for data manipulation","A document storage system with no structured query capability available"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per the ANSI/ISO SQL standard (ISO/IEC 9075), an RDBMS organizes data into structured tables linked through primary and foreign key relationships and uses SQL for data manipulation. This architecture enforces referential integrity and supports complex queries across related tables.
Wrong (A): Flat file systems store data in unstructured text without defined relationships or SQL query capability, lacking the referential integrity the controller requires.
Wrong (B): Spreadsheet applications support financial analysis but lack the relational data model, ACID compliance, and multi-user transaction processing of an RDBMS.
Wrong (D): Document storage systems without query capability cannot enforce data relationships or support the structured queries needed for an accounting system.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3501;

-- Q3503: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A referential integrity constraint using a foreign key relationship","A check constraint validating values against defined conditions","A unique constraint preventing duplicate values in a column","A not-null constraint requiring a value in every row"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): A foreign key constraint enforces referential integrity by requiring every value in the child column to match an existing value in the parent table, per COBIT 2019 DSS06.02. This prevents orphaned records and ensures valid relationships between tables.
Wrong (B): A check constraint validates values against specific conditions such as numeric ranges or allowed values, but cannot verify that a referenced row exists in another table.
Wrong (C): A unique constraint ensures no duplicate values exist within a column but does not enforce cross-table referential relationships between parent and child records.
Wrong (D): A not-null constraint requires that a column contain a value in every row but does not validate whether that value corresponds to an existing record in another table.$EXPL$
