-- Q3504: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Stronger enforcement of ACID transaction properties across all operations","Flexible schema accommodating varying data structures without predefined tables","Better support for complex SQL JOIN operations across normalized tables","Mandatory data normalization enforced automatically across all stored documents"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT 2019 APO03.02, a NoSQL document database stores data as flexible JSON-like documents where each document can have a different structure. This schema flexibility accommodates varying content types without requiring a rigid predefined schema.
Wrong (A): Relational databases provide stronger ACID transaction enforcement. Most NoSQL document stores trade strict ACID compliance for scalability and schema flexibility.
Wrong (C): SQL JOIN operations are a strength of relational databases, not NoSQL document stores, which typically denormalize data to avoid cross-collection joins.
Wrong (D): NoSQL document databases do not enforce normalization. Their advantage is the opposite: allowing denormalized, flexible structures suited to unstructured data.$EXPL$
