-- Q3510: Data Management and Lifecycle [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A central fact table with business metrics surrounded by descriptive dimension tables","A single denormalized table containing all organizational data in one structure","A fully normalized set of tables designed in third normal form for OLTP","A collection of unrelated flat files stored in a shared directory"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per COBIT 2019 APO03, a star schema organizes a data warehouse with a central fact table containing quantitative measures surrounded by denormalized dimension tables providing descriptive context. The fact table''s foreign keys connect to dimension tables for date, product, customer, and geography.
Wrong (B): A single denormalized table would lack the dimensional separation that enables efficient analytical queries and the organized structure characteristic of a star schema.
Wrong (C): A fully normalized third normal form design is optimized for transactional integrity in OLTP systems, not for the analytical query performance a star schema provides.
Wrong (D): Unrelated flat files have no defined relationships or structure. A star schema is a purposefully designed relational model with a central fact table and linked dimensions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3510;

-- Q3511: Data Management and Lifecycle [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["OLAP handles individual transactions while OLTP performs bulk analytical queries","OLAP and OLTP are identical technologies serving the same business purpose","OLAP handles complex historical queries while OLTP handles fast transaction processing","OLAP only works with spreadsheets while OLTP only works with relational databases"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per COBIT 2019 APO03, OLAP systems are purpose-built for complex aggregate queries across large volumes of historical data, supporting dimensional modeling and read-heavy analytical workloads. OLTP systems are optimized for fast concurrent transaction processing with ACID compliance.
Wrong (A): This reverses the roles. OLTP handles individual transactions, while OLAP performs bulk analytical queries across historical data.
Wrong (B): OLAP and OLTP serve fundamentally different purposes. OLAP supports analytical decision-making, while OLTP supports day-to-day operational transaction processing.
Wrong (D): Both OLAP and OLTP can work with relational databases. OLAP is not limited to spreadsheets; it uses specialized multidimensional data structures.$EXPL$
