-- Q3518: Data Management and Lifecycle [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reformatting is the most secure data destruction method currently available","Reformatting destroys data more thoroughly than physical destruction methods","Financial data on decommissioned drives does not require secure destruction","Reformatting only removes file system references, leaving data forensically recoverable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-88, standard reformatting only removes file system metadata without overwriting actual data blocks on the disk. The underlying information remains fully recoverable by forensic tools from residual magnetic patterns on the platters.
Wrong (A): Reformatting is among the least secure methods. Secure destruction requires cryptographic erasure, degaussing, multi-pass overwriting, or physical shredding per NIST SP 800-88.
Wrong (B): Physical destruction (shredding, incineration) renders data completely unrecoverable, making it far more thorough than reformatting, which preserves underlying data.
Wrong (C): Financial data often contains sensitive information subject to regulatory retention and secure disposal requirements. Inadequate destruction creates data breach risk.$EXPL$
