-- Q3529: Data Management and Lifecycle [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Degaussing is too expensive to be practical for solid-state drive media","SSDs use electrical charges in flash memory, so magnetic degaussing has no effect","Degaussing works perfectly on SSDs and is the recommended sanitization method","SSDs cannot store sensitive customer data requiring secure destruction procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, degaussing is effective only for magnetic storage media because it uses strong magnetic fields to erase data. SSDs store data using electrical charges in NAND flash memory cells, which are completely unaffected by magnetic fields.
Wrong (A): Cost is not the reason degaussing is insufficient for SSDs. The fundamental issue is that degaussing physically cannot affect the non-magnetic flash memory technology SSDs use.
Wrong (C): Degaussing has zero effect on SSDs. NIST SP 800-88 recommends cryptographic erasure, ATA Secure Erase, or physical destruction for SSD sanitization.
Wrong (D): SSDs routinely store sensitive data including financial records. The question is about the appropriate destruction method, not the drive''s capability to store sensitive information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3529;

-- Q3531: Data Management and Lifecycle [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the discrepancy if the amount falls below the established materiality level","Run a reconciliation query to isolate the specific records causing the difference","Assume the general ledger is correct and adjust the subledger to force agreement","Delete the entire subledger and recreate it using only general ledger balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA Trust Services Criteria PI1.4, data discrepancies require systematic investigation to identify root causes. The auditor should first perform a detailed reconciliation to isolate specific records causing the $47,000 difference, revealing whether the issue stems from unposted batches, data entry errors, or unauthorized adjustments.
Wrong (A): Ignoring a discrepancy without investigation violates audit standards. Even below-materiality differences may indicate control weaknesses or systematic errors requiring attention.
Wrong (C): Assuming either system is correct without investigation bypasses root cause analysis. The GL and subledger may both contain errors requiring different corrections.
Wrong (D): Deleting and recreating the subledger destroys audit evidence and transaction history. It also fails to identify the root cause, which may recur without proper investigation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3531;
